          <p>
            The Original powder is lean: 20 g of protein in a 25.9 g scoop means about 77% of the powder by weight is protein, which is typical for a whey concentrate blend. The 2 g of sugar is naturally occurring milk sugar. Chocolate is a different proposition. Its 46.7 g scoop carries the same 20 g of protein but 170 calories and 13 g of sugar, 12 g of which is added coconut sugar, so protein drops to about 43% of the powder by weight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Milk protein concentrate means casein</h3>

          <p>
            Milk protein concentrate keeps milk's natural ratio of roughly 80% casein to 20% whey, so Lineage ABC is a casein and whey blend, not a pure whey. That gives it a slower, more sustained digestion than straight whey, which some people prefer for a meal replacement. It also means anyone avoiding casein specifically should look elsewhere. Our <a href="/casein-vs-whey-protein-powder/">casein vs whey</a> guide explains the trade-off.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two concentrates also contain some lactose. Lineage says milk and whey protein concentrates are naturally low in lactose and that many people who struggle with dairy do fine with them, but it does not publish a lactose figure, and it is clear that anyone with a true milk allergy should not use the product. If lactose is your issue, a <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> built on isolate is a safer bet. The product page lists milk and tree nuts (coconut) as allergens.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Lineage Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes. Lineage Provisions says every batch of its products is tested by Light Labs, an ISO/IEC 17025 accredited laboratory, and that it publishes the certificates of analysis (COAs) on its product pages. The brand says Animal-Based Complete is screened for more than 300 contaminants, including heavy metals, glyphosate, mycotoxins, pesticides and herbicides.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lineage's testing page lists the parameters it covers: heavy metals, potency and label claims, microbials, pesticides, glyphosate and its breakdown product AMPA, mycotoxins, solvent residues, non-GMO status and microplastics. It describes the methods as ICP-MS, LC-MS and GC-MS, which are the standard instruments for metals and chemical residues. Publishing batch results by lot is more than most premium protein brands do, and it lets you check the numbers rather than trusting a badge.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT THE TESTING IS, AND IS NOT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Batch testing is not a sport certification</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Light Labs testing is commissioned by Lineage and published by Lineage. It is genuine third-party lab work, but it is not the same as NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport, which screen for banned substances and audit the facility. Lineage does not claim either certification. Athletes in drug-tested sport should choose a product with one of those marks.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two practical caveats. First, a COA shows results for specific lots, so match the lot number on your bag to the report if you want to know what you are drinking. Second, chocolate powders as a category tend to carry more lead than unflavored ones because cocoa absorbs it from soil; if heavy metals are your main concern, the unflavored Original is the safer default. Our guide to <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> explains how to read these reports.
          </p>

          <p>
            We did not come across independent heavy-metal results for Lineage Animal-Based Complete from another organization at the time of writing, so the brand's own published Light Labs reports are the contaminant data to check. Ask Lineage at its support address for a COA if the current lot is not posted.
          </p>
        </div>

