        <div id="taste-and-mixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Do Buyers Say About Lineage Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers mostly rate Lineage highly: its own product page showed about 4.9 stars from roughly 2,000 reviews at the time of writing, though reviews hosted on a brand's site are moderated by the brand and may include the earlier beef formula. The most common complaint, which Lineage itself acknowledges, is clumping when the powder is stirred with a spoon.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Taste</h3>

          <p>
            Lineage describes the Original as mild and neutral with no added flavor, and buyers commonly use it in coffee, smoothies, yogurt and baking rather than plain water. The question many shoppers ask is whether the organs make it taste like liver. Lineage says no, and most buyer feedback agrees, but at least one reviewer on Lineage's own page reported a slight liver note in the chocolate version mixed with coconut milk alone, while noting a sensitive palate. Chocolate gets the most praise for drinking straight, which makes sense given its 12 g of coconut sugar.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mixing</h3>

          <p>
            Animal-Based Complete has no lecithin, gums or emulsifiers, so it does not disperse as easily as most commercial whey. Lineage's FAQ says plainly that it clumps if you stir it with a spoon, and recommends a blender bottle, a blender or the battery frother it sells for $19. Its support pages say hand mixing or shaking will be difficult. If you mainly drink shakes on the go, factor that in; a <a href="/best-portable-blender-for-protein-shakes/">portable blender</a> solves it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lineage offers a 100% money-back guarantee on the powder, per its product page, which lowers the risk if you are unsure about the taste. Buyers who are used to sweet, thick commercial shakes are the group most likely to be disappointed by the unflavored version.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="organs-colostrum" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Do the Organs and Colostrum in Lineage Make a Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The organ blend and colostrum in Lineage are present in small amounts: 800 mg of mixed beef organs and 250 mg of colostrum per scoop, per the label. That is less than one gram of organs, against roughly 85 g in a typical serving of liver, so the protein in each scoop comes almost entirely from the milk and whey concentrates.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lineage says the liver and heart deliver B12, iron and CoQ10, but the Original supplement facts panel does not list amounts for vitamin B12 or iron, so we cannot tell you how much you get. The chocolate panel lists 2.1 mg of iron, some of which is likely to come from cocoa. Treat the organ blend as a nod to the nose-to-tail philosophy rather than a meaningful dose of any vitamin.
          </p>

          <p>
            Colostrum research is mixed and mostly uses doses of several grams a day, well above the 250 mg in a scoop of ABC. Lineage says colostrum supports gut health and immune function, a claim it makes as a supplement brand, not one this dose has been tested for. If colostrum is the reason you are buying, a dedicated colostrum powder gives you far more per dollar.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes Lineage a bad product. The core is a clean, grass-fed dairy protein with published testing, which is a reasonable thing to pay extra for. Just buy it for that, not for the add-ins. People who are pregnant, managing kidney disease, taking medication or with any medical condition should check with a doctor or registered dietitian before adding organ or colostrum supplements, as with any protein powder.
          </p>
        </div>

