        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Lineage Protein Powder, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Lineage protein powder suits people who want an unsweetened, gum-free, grass-fed dairy protein with published batch testing and are happy to pay a premium for it. It is a poor fit for budget shoppers, dairy-free eaters and athletes who need a sport certification.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Good fit</h3>

          <p>
            Animal-based and ancestral-diet eaters who already buy Saladino's approach will find the label lines up with what they want: grass-fed dairy, organ meat, colostrum and no seed oils, gums or artificial sweeteners. It also suits anyone who wants an <a href="/best-unflavored-protein-powder/">unflavored protein powder</a> for cooking and coffee, and shoppers who value <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein without artificial sweeteners</a> and posted lab results over price.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Poor fit</h3>

          <p>
            Skip Lineage if you want the most protein per dollar; you can get grass-fed whey for half the price. Skip it if you have a milk allergy or need a dairy-free protein, since every version contains milk. Skip it if you compete in drug-tested sport and need NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport. And skip the chocolate version if you are watching sugar, since 12 g of added sugar per scoop is more than many sweetened drinks per serving.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most shoppers, the sensible plan is to buy the Original once, through Amazon or with the money-back guarantee on Lineage's site, and decide whether the neutral taste and spoon-clumping fit your routine before committing to a subscription.
          </p>
        </div>

