        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming Lineage is still a beef protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lineage launched Animal-Based Complete as a grass-fed beef protein, and many older reviews describe that formula. The version it now leads with, and sells on Amazon, is a dairy protein made from milk and whey protein concentrates. Check the formula on the bag or listing before buying, especially if you chose it to avoid whey.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying it as a dairy-free protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Even Lineage's beef formula contains bovine colostrum, which is a milk ingredient, and its label declares milk. No version of Animal-Based Complete is dairy-free. If you have a milk allergy or follow a strict dairy-free diet, choose a beef isolate such as Equip Prime or a plant protein instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stirring it with a spoon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Animal-Based Complete has no gums, lecithin or emulsifiers, so it clumps when stirred in a glass. Lineage itself recommends a blender bottle, blender or frother. Buyers who skip that step are the ones most likely to complain about texture, and the clumps are a mixing issue, not a sign the powder has gone bad.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a real dose of organs or colostrum</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Each scoop has 800 mg of mixed organs and 250 mg of colostrum, less than a gram of each. That is a small fraction of a serving of liver and well below the multi-gram colostrum doses used in most studies. Buy Lineage for the clean grass-fed protein, not as a replacement for eating organ meat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating batch testing as an NSF certification</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lineage's Light Labs testing is real third-party lab work with published COAs, but it is commissioned by the brand and does not screen for banned substances in the way NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport do. Drug-tested athletes need one of those certifications, which Lineage does not claim.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

