              <summary><h3>Is Lineage third-party tested?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Lineage Provisions says every batch of Animal-Based Complete is tested by Light Labs, an ISO 17025 accredited lab, for more than 300 contaminants including heavy metals, glyphosate, mycotoxins and pesticides, and it posts the certificates of analysis on its product pages. It does not claim NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification, so it is not the right pick for drug-tested athletes.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Lineage protein powder healthy and clean?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  By label, yes: the Original contains grass-fed milk and whey protein concentrate, sea salt and small organ, colostrum, berry and coconut water blends, with no sweetener, gums, fillers or artificial flavors. It gives 20 g of protein for 100 calories. The Chocolate version adds 12 g of coconut sugar per scoop, which is the main thing to watch if you are limiting added sugar.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Lineage protein contain casein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. The current dairy formula uses grass-fed milk protein concentrate, which naturally contains roughly 80% casein and 20% whey, alongside whey protein concentrate. That makes Lineage a casein and whey blend rather than a pure whey. The beef formula still offered on Lineage's site uses beef protein isolate instead, but it contains colostrum, so it is not milk-free either.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does Lineage protein powder taste like liver?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Lineage says no, and most buyer feedback describes the Original as mild and neutral. The organ blend is only 800 mg per scoop, which helps. A small number of reviewers report a faint organ note, for example in the chocolate version mixed with coconut milk alone. Buyers who blend it into coffee, smoothies or yogurt report the fewest taste issues.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which Lineage flavor is best?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Buyer feedback favors Chocolate for drinking with just water or milk, since it is sweetened with organic coconut sugar and cocoa. The Original is the more popular choice for coffee, smoothies and baking because it has no flavor or sweetener. If you are watching sugar, choose Original: Chocolate has 13 g of sugar and 170 calories per scoop, against 2 g and 100.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What are the common complaints about Lineage protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  The most common complaints are clumping when stirred with a spoon, which Lineage attributes to having no gums or emulsifiers, and the price of about $3 per 25 g of protein. Some buyers also note 20 g of protein per scoop is lower than most whey, and a few mention a faint organ taste. Lineage offers a money-back guarantee if you dislike it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can people with lactose intolerance use Lineage protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Possibly, but check with care. Lineage says milk and whey protein concentrates are naturally low in lactose and that many dairy-sensitive people tolerate them, but it does not publish a lactose figure. Anyone with a true milk allergy should avoid it entirely. If lactose bothers you, an isolate-based or plant protein is a safer choice; ask a doctor or dietitian if unsure.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

